{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779211790,"nonce":"qlXV7buZEc64/gLxLS0c9WS7Z3s=","title":"Spec: composable task timelines and expression-valued parameters","message":"## Problem\n\nStudio currently represents delayed-task structure and many task parameters as raw values or JSON-ish arrays in generic fields. That is not a durable UX for tasks like delayed reaches, eval overrides, perturbations, temporal loss weights, acausal component parameters, or trial/replicate variation.\n\nThe product needs a generalized authoring system for values that are sometimes constants, sometimes expressions, sometimes reusable time-varying functions, and sometimes sampled/random variables. This should apply first to task timelines and signals, but the same concept should be reusable for component properties, acausal-system parameters, objective weights, eval overrides, and sweep/replicate definitions.\n\nThis issue is intentionally cross-cutting. It should guide child implementation slices rather than be treated as a single widget polish task.\n\n## Precedents\n\n- Simulink Signal Editor separates scenario selection from signal authoring. It supports multiple scenarios, multiple signals, MATLAB-expression signal notation, multidimensional signals, signal properties such as units and interpolation, and one output port per signal. See MathWorks Signal Editor docs: https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/slref/signaleditorblock.html\n- PsychoPy Builder represents experiments as routines/components with explicit onset/duration conditions. Component fields can be literal values, variables, or code-like expressions, and variable duration still gets an expected duration for the visual routine view. See PsychoPy Builder start/stop and components docs: https://docs.psychopy.org/builder/startStop.html and https://psychopy.org/builder/components/index.html\n- COMSOL uses a shared function library concept: Analytic, Interpolation, and Piecewise functions, common templates such as step/ramp, global or component-local scope, and use of those functions anywhere parameters can be used. See COMSOL user-defined functions: https://doc.comsol.com/6.4/doc/com.comsol.help.comsol/comsol_ref_definitions.21.057.html\n- CAD systems treat expression-valued fields as normal. Onshape variables can be referenced inside numeric fields with autocomplete and scoped availability; Autodesk Fusion parameter expressions support units, order of operations, constants, and logical functions. See Onshape Variables and Fusion Parameters: https://cad.onshape.com/help/Content/PartStudio/variable.htm and https://help.autodesk.com/view/fusion360/ENU/?guid=GUID-76272551-3275-46C4-AE4D-10D58B408C20\n- Houdini ramp parameters are a useful precedent for reusable one-dimensional functional shapes: editable points, interpolation, preset configurations, and expression access. See SideFX ramp parameters: https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/network/ramps.html\n- W\u0026B Sweeps shows the ML-side vocabulary for values that are constants, categorical values, bounded distributions, quantized distributions, nested parameters, and grid/random/Bayesian exploration. See W\u0026B sweep configuration options: https://docs.wandb.ai/models/sweeps/sweep-config-keys\n- Modelica and Simscape are useful boundary precedents for acausal systems: component parameters, units, equations, physical connections, and reusable component libraries must remain semantically typed rather than becoming arbitrary UI-local strings. See Modelica language overview and Simscape physical modeling techniques: https://modelica.org/language/ and https://www.mathworks.com/help/simscape/physical-modeling-techniques.html\n- KNIME expression nodes show the data-science pattern: expression editors are typed against available columns/variables and become graph nodes with clear inputs/outputs rather than free-floating text in unrelated forms. See KNIME Expressions Guide: https://docs.knime.com/ap/latest/knime_expressions_guide/index.html\n\n## Design Direction\n\nIntroduce a shared `ValueSpec` / `ExpressionValueSpec` concept that can appear anywhere Studio currently shows a scalar/vector/object field. It should support at least:\n\n- `constant`: literal scalar/vector/array/tree value with units, dtype, shape, axis labels, and frame metadata where available.\n- `reference`: named parameter, task attribute, signal, selector path, artifact field, or upstream stage output.\n- `expression`: typed expression over allowed variables, with autocomplete and validation. Raw text is allowed but should not be the primary beginner path.\n- `function`: reusable shape/template, such as step, ramp, pulse, sigmoid, exponential decay, piecewise, interpolation table, hold, target trajectory, noise process, or imported backend function.\n- `distribution`: sampled value with distribution family, parameters, support, quantization, seed policy, and sampling scope.\n- `schedule`: value that changes over time, epoch, batch, training step, trial, replicate, or run.\n\nThe UI should render the current mode as a compact chip/control in ordinary property fields, e.g. `0.5`, `Normal(0.5, 0.1) per trial`, `ramp(\"go_cue\")`, `target_position(t)`, or `#workspace_radius * 0.5`. Editing the chip opens the appropriate structured editor.\n\n## Task Timeline Editor\n\nDelayed tasks should get a dedicated timeline/signal editor rather than text fields containing arrays.\n\nA first useful editor can be a grid:\n\n- Columns are named epochs/phases, such as `pre-go`, `go/target`, `move/hold`, with add/remove/reorder controls.\n- Each epoch has a length spec shown above or below the grid: fixed length, integer range, distribution, expression, or event/adaptive end condition.\n- Rows are signals/targets/interventions, such as `target`, `go cue`, `hold cue`, `SISU`, `perturbation`, `initial state`, or project-defined channels.\n- A boolean lane can use checkboxes for simple on/off presence.\n- Non-boolean lanes use cells containing value/function chips. Clicking a cell opens a structured editor for that epoch's signal value.\n- The editor previews generated signals over time and can show sampled examples for ranges/distributions.\n- Signals have definitions outside the grid: name, semantic role, dtype/shape, units/frame, default value, backend binding, and visibility on the graph/inspector.\n\nFor the current delayed reach fixture, the UI should represent the three-phase structure directly: epoch length ranges, target-present epochs, go/hold cue epochs, movement/hold semantics, catch-trial probability, endpoint generation, and validation/eval variants. The user should not have to interpret `epoch_len_ranges`, `target_on_epochs`, or similar array fields.\n\n## Reusable Function Shapes\n\nStudio should have a project-level function/template library, with optional component-local definitions.\n\nFunctions should include common presets at first:\n\n- constant, step, pulse, ramp, trapezoid, piecewise linear, sigmoid/logistic, exponential decay, sinusoid;\n- interpolation/table lookup;\n- noise or stochastic process wrappers;\n- target trajectory constructors;\n- perturbation/intervention profiles.\n\nEach function should declare input axes (`t`, normalized phase progress, trial attributes, task params, state selector values, etc.), output type/shape/units, default parameterization, and preview behavior. Instances can scale or bind parameters differently without duplicating the template. This follows the COMSOL/Houdini pattern: define reusable functions once, use them in many fields.\n\n## Expressions Without Turning the UI Into Code\n\nThe default editor should be structured:\n\n- variable/reference picker with search and autocomplete;\n- function palette with typed signatures;\n- unit/shape/dtype validation while editing;\n- quick visual preview for time-varying functions;\n- dependency display: what this value references and which downstream specs depend on it;\n- raw expression mode only as an advanced escape hatch.\n\nThe durable representation should be an AST or typed declarative spec, not arbitrary frontend strings. This lets the backend validate, lower to JAX-compatible callables, serialize into manifests, and report errors against spec paths.\n\n## Distributions and Sampling Scope\n\nA distribution is not only a sweep. Studio needs explicit sampling scope:\n\n- once per project/spec snapshot;\n- once per run;\n- once per replicate;\n- once per trial;\n- once per epoch;\n- once per timestep, if allowed;\n- grid/sweep values across runs.\n\nThe UI should show scope in the field itself. Example: `Uniform(0.4, 0.6) per trial`, `Categorical([0, 1]) per catch trial`, `LogUniform(1e-4, 1e-2) per run`. Seeds and RNG streams must be part of the lowered spec/manifests so results are reproducible.\n\n## Graph and Acausal-System Relationship\n\nNot every variable should become a graph port. Some values are compile-time/model parameters; some are trial-varying task controls; some are runtime signals; some are eval-stage overrides. The UI should make that distinction visible.\n\nRules of thumb:\n\n- If a value changes over time inside a rollout and participates in model dynamics, consider representing it as a signal/port or task channel.\n- If a value varies by trial/replicate/run but is static during a rollout, show it as a sampled parameter with explicit scope.\n- If a hidden property starts acquiring dependencies or runtime variation, offer `Promote to input/port` or `Promote to scenario control` rather than silently keeping it buried in a property editor.\n- Acausal-system fields should use the same `ValueSpec` widget, but with stronger unit/domain validation and equation/connector semantics aligned with `a6efafc`, `6116155`, and core backend validators.\n\n## Implementation Slices\n\n1. Core spec: define `ValueSpec` / `ExpressionValueSpec`, function templates, references, units/shapes/types, distribution specs, and sampling scopes.\n2. Delayed-task editor: replace raw delayed-reach array fields with epoch columns, signal rows, length controls, and preview.\n3. Function library: add reusable function templates and instance parameterization.\n4. Expression editor: autocomplete over task variables, graph ports, selectors, project parameters, and function templates; validate without running backend code.\n5. Backend lowering/validation: deterministic lowering to task generation, JAX-compatible functions, manifests, and spec-path errors.\n6. Property-field adoption: use the same value widget for component properties, objective weights, eval overrides, acausal parameters, and sweep/replicate fields.\n7. Graph promotion: allow property values to become visible scenario controls or graph ports when their scope/dependencies make that more honest.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\nA first serious implementation should satisfy:\n\n- Delayed-reach task parameters no longer appear primarily as raw array/object text fields in Studio.\n- The RLRMP delayed center-out example can be inspected as epochs plus signal lanes, including length ranges, target/go/hold semantics, catch trials, and endpoint generation.\n- Any parameter field can explicitly represent constant, reference, expression, reusable function, distribution, or schedule modes.\n- Time-varying functions have previews, reusable templates, and validated units/shapes.\n- Distribution-valued fields show sampling scope and lower into deterministic seed/manifest metadata.\n- Eval-stage overrides such as SISU, perturbation profile, and task variant can use the same system instead of hardcoded labels.\n- Acausal/component property editors can reuse the value editor without losing unit/domain semantics.\n- Backend validation returns structured errors tied to spec paths and visible UI objects.\n- Raw code/expression text remains possible for advanced users, but common cases are authored through structured controls.\n\n## Non-Goals for the First Slice\n\n- Full arbitrary Python/JAX function editing in the browser.\n- A complete symbolic equation solver for acausal systems.\n- Solving runtime PyTree schema introspection; this issue should interoperate with `85cec2c`, not replace it.\n- Replacing all existing component property forms in one pass.\n\n## Related Issues\n\n- `93f79f2` — Feedbax Studio design coordination.\n- `8d94424` — Feedbax design coordination.\n- `0c46f69` — Studio pipeline workspace; eval stages, task variants, overrides, stage-owned specs.\n- `c6c6da0` — provider/manifest contract; durable schema and validation boundary.\n- `d64a9aa` — environment/task protocol unification.\n- `c540499` — lazy task generation / compact task params.\n- `3fc3e8e` — adaptive-length trials.\n- `85cec2c` — runtime/schema-backed state browser for selectors.\n- `b3ad156` — flexible state targeting/interventions.\n- `a6efafc` and `6116155` — acausal subgraphs and typed subgraph/canvas architecture.\n- `e57ae1f` — real backend lowering/execution.\n- `e5a1e6c` — metric specs and task-aware metric columns.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}